WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.528
Preventing copies is more reliable than
cleaning them up later.

2
00:00:04.528 --> 00:00:08.077
Keep production credentials out of source
files, committed environment

3
00:00:08.077 --> 00:00:12.105
files, sample configuration, fixtures,
build scripts, and troubleshooting output.

4
00:00:12.105 --> 00:00:15.654
Local development environment files can be
useful, but credential-bearing

5
00:00:15.654 --> 00:00:19.299
files belong outside version control with
appropriate access controls.

6
00:00:19.299 --> 00:00:21.601
Samples should name required settings
using

7
00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:23.711
empty values or unmistakable placeholders.

8
00:00:23.711 --> 00:00:26.108
Environment variables are a delivery
mechanism,

9
00:00:26.108 --> 00:00:28.602
not a secret-management system or
encryption.

10
00:00:28.602 --> 00:00:31.480
Their protection depends on provisioning,
process isolation,

11
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.549
inspection privileges, deployment tooling,
and operational controls.

12
00:00:34.549 --> 00:00:37.042
A child process may inherit them.

13
00:00:37.042 --> 00:00:40.303
Crash collection and process dumps may
capture them.

14
00:00:40.303 --> 00:00:43.756
Review what each process receives and what
diagnostic systems

15
00:00:43.756 --> 00:00:46.825
retain instead of assuming either that all
environment

16
00:00:46.825 --> 00:00:50.470
variables are safe or that they are always
prohibited.

17
00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:52.580
Build systems create another boundary.

18
00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.649
Container build arguments and copied files
can expose

19
00:00:55.649 --> 00:00:59.102
credentials in image layers, build
records, or caches.

20
00:00:59.102 --> 00:01:01.788
Removing a file in a later layer

21
00:01:01.788 --> 00:01:04.665
does not erase it from earlier layers.

22
00:01:04.665 --> 00:01:08.118
Use the build platform's supported
ephemeral secret mechanism when

23
00:01:08.118 --> 00:01:11.667
a build needs authentication, and ensure
the command does

24
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:15.311
not copy or print the value into an
artifact.

25
00:01:15.311 --> 00:01:17.805
Browser JavaScript is delivered to users.

26
00:01:17.805 --> 00:01:20.874
A frontend variable called SECRET_KEY is
visible if

27
00:01:20.874 --> 00:01:24.231
bundled into the application, including
through build-time substitution.

28
00:01:24.231 --> 00:01:28.259
Source maps and generated static files can
expose additional content.

29
00:01:28.259 --> 00:01:31.425
Put operations requiring confidential
credentials behind an authenticated,

30
00:01:31.425 --> 00:01:34.973
authorized server endpoint; validate the
requested operation so that

31
00:01:34.973 --> 00:01:38.234
endpoint does not become an unrestricted
credential proxy.

32
00:01:38.234 --> 00:01:40.728
Telemetry must follow the same boundary.

33
00:01:40.728 --> 00:01:44.085
Do not log tokens, passwords, API keys,
private

34
00:01:44.085 --> 00:01:47.154
keys, authorization headers, or complete
credential objects.

35
00:01:47.154 --> 00:01:50.319
Prefer an allowlist of safe fields, such
as

36
00:01:50.319 --> 00:01:53.197
an approved key identifier or credential
version.

37
00:01:53.197 --> 00:01:56.458
Sanitize SDK errors and request traces
before collection.

38
00:01:56.458 --> 00:01:59.623
Redaction helps, but structured
diagnostics that never accept

39
00:01:59.623 --> 00:02:02.500
secret values are easier to reason about.

